<node name="/" xmlns:doc="http://www.freedesktop.org/dbus/1.0/doc.dtd">
  <!--
      org.freedesktop.portal.experimental.WebAuthentication:
      @short_description: Portal for completing a web sign-in flow

      This interface lets an application hand a web sign-in flow to the
      desktop. The application says where the flow starts and which URI ends
      it; the portal opens a web view it controls, and answers with the URI
      the flow finished on. Everything in between - cookies, redirects,
      client certificate prompts, PIN entry - happens outside the application.

      The application never sees the web view, and the portal never gives the
      application anything but the completion URI it asked for: the completion
      URI in the response is checked against the one that was requested before
      it is handed over.

      This is not a browser and not a general purpose web view. It exists so
      that an application does not have to embed one in order to sign in.

      This is an **experimental** interface. It is not exported unless
      ``XDG_DESKTOP_PORTAL_ENABLE_EXPERIMENTAL`` contains
      ``web-authentication``, and it can change or get removed without a
      version bump.

      This documentation describes version 1 of this interface.
  -->
  <interface name="org.freedesktop.portal.experimental.WebAuthentication">
    <!--
        Start:
        @parent_window: Identifier for the application window, see :doc:`window-identifiers`
        @start_uri: The URI to open
        @completion_uri: The URI whose navigation ends the flow
        @options: Vardict with optional further information
        @handle: Object path for the :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Request` object representing this call

        Starts a web sign-in flow.

        A sandboxed application that has no network access cannot call this
        method: the response would be an answer fetched from the network, so
        a sandbox which is not allowed to reach the network must not be able
        to reach it through the portal either. The call fails with
        ``org.freedesktop.portal.Error.NotAllowed``.

        @start_uri must be an absolute ``https`` URI with a host.

        @completion_uri must be an absolute URI with no userinfo. An ``http``
        or ``https`` completion URI must name a host. A private-use scheme
        redirect URI as described in :rfc:`8252` section 7.1 - for example
        ``com.example.app:/oauth2redirect`` - has no authority at all, and is
        accepted with only a path; it is then matched on its scheme and its
        path. A completion URI which names neither a host nor a path is
        refused, because there would be nothing to match on.

        A wildcard is not a pattern here and is refused: a host containing
        ``*`` is a D-Bus error, not a match against every subdomain.

        @completion_uri is matched against every navigation the web view is
        asked to make - **in any frame**, not only the top level one. Both
        URIs are parsed before they are compared, so the comparison is on
        components and not on the two strings:

        * the scheme and the host are compared case insensitively, and a URI
          with a host never matches one without;
        * the port is compared with the default port of the scheme filled in,
          so ``https://example.com/x`` and ``https://example.com:443/x``
          are the same URI, and a private-use scheme has no default port;
        * the path is compared exactly, on the form GLib's URI parser
          produces;
        * the query and the fragment carry the result of the flow and take no
          part in matching.

        Parsing normalises the path and the host, so these are equal and all
        of them match: ``/%63allback`` and ``/callback``, because an escape
        which does not have to be one is decoded; ``/call%2fback`` and
        ``/call%2Fback``, because an escape which does have to be one keeps
        uppercase hex; ``/a/../callback``, ``/./callback`` and ``/callback``,
        because dot segments are resolved; and an internationalised host and
        its punycode spelling, because the host is converted to punycode.

        Two differences that look like equivalences are not, and both fail
        closed: an empty path and ``/`` are different paths, so
        ``https://example.com`` never matches ``https://example.com/``; and a
        host with a trailing dot is a different host.

        A match ends the flow and **is never loaded**: the matched URI carries
        the result of the sign-in, and fetching it would send that result to a
        page which has no part in the exchange. This is the rule in both
        directions - a subframe cannot quietly fetch the completion URI, and a
        subframe navigation to it does end the flow rather than being ignored.
        Backends are not asked to tell frames apart, because engines do not
        reliably expose that at the moment the decision must be made.

        A malformed request is a D-Bus error from this method, before any
        backend is asked and any window is opened.

        Supported keys in the @options vardict include:

        * ``handle_token`` (``s``)

          A string that will be used as the last element of the @handle. Must be a valid
          object path element. See the :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Request` documentation for
          more information about the @handle.

        * ``activation_token`` (``s``)

          A token that can be used to activate the window, see
          :doc:`window-identifiers`.

        * ``session_mode`` (``s``)

          Which web storage the flow uses: ``shared`` (the default) or
          ``ephemeral``. This is a web storage mode, not an
          :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Session` - this interface creates no
          session object. ``shared`` means shared between flows run by this
          backend, never with the user's browser. ``ephemeral`` means a data
          store made for this flow and destroyed with it. An unknown value is
          an error, not a fallback.

        * ``timeout`` (``u``)

          How long, in seconds, the flow may take. The default is 300 and any
          value above the ceiling of 900 is clamped to it. The portal keeps
          this deadline: when it passes, the portal closes the backend's
          request so that the window goes away, and the response is ``2``
          with ``reason`` ``timeout``. The value is passed on to the backend
          as well, which may end the flow sooner. An application that wants
          to give up before then calls
          :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Request.Close`.

        * ``title`` (``s``)

          A short hint about what the sign-in is for. This is
          application-supplied text and is shown as such, never as the
          identity of the window.

        The following results get returned via the :ref:`org.freedesktop.portal.Request::Response` signal:

        * ``completion_uri`` (``s``)

          The matched URI, complete and undecoded, including query and
          fragment. Present only when the response is ``0``. The portal
          re-checks this value against the @completion_uri it forwarded before
          emitting it; if it does not match, the response becomes ``2`` with
          ``reason`` ``backend_completion_mismatch`` and the URI is discarded.

        * ``reason`` (``s``)

          An optional stable symbol explaining a non-zero response. The portal
          itself emits ``timeout``, ``backend_disappeared``,
          ``backend_completion_mismatch`` and ``backend_protocol_error``, and
          forwards whatever the backend gave, for instance ``timeout``,
          ``no_display``, ``user_cancelled`` or ``credential_unavailable``. It
          is a diagnostic, never a substitute for the response code, and
          consumers must tolerate its absence and values they do not know.
    -->
    <method name="Start">
      <arg type="s" name="parent_window" direction="in"/>
      <arg type="s" name="start_uri" direction="in"/>
      <arg type="s" name="completion_uri" direction="in"/>
      <annotation name="org.qtproject.QtDBus.QtTypeName.In3" value="QVariantMap"/>
      <arg type="a{sv}" name="options" direction="in"/>
      <arg type="o" name="handle" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <property name="version" type="u" access="read"/>
  </interface>
</node>
